From 7f9e031da2350f5999a615fb5fb266a6a489c764 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Micha=C5=82=20=C5=BBygowski?= <michal.zygowski@3mdeb.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 15:20:08 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] soc/intel/lockdown: Allow locking down SPI and LPC in SMM
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Heads payload uses APM_CNT_FINALIZE SMI to set and lock down the SPI
controller with PR0 flash protection for pre-Skylake platforms.

Add an option to defer LPC and FAST SPI lockdown to the APM_CNT_FINALIZE
SMI handler. Reuse INTEL_CHIPSET_LOCKDOWN to avoid issuing
APM_CNT_FINALIZE on the normal boot path, as on pre-Skylake platforms.
Split SPI and LPC lockdown into separate modules so they can be linked into
SMM.

Defer boot-media protection as part of that mode. At finalization, reuse an
exact whole-ROM FPR or program and verify a free register before setting
DLOCK and FLOCKDN. Report setup failures through APM_STS instead of halting
inside SMM.

Make the SMM mode use coreboot lockdown ownership even when the mainboard
normally selects FSP lockdown. Otherwise FSP can lock empty protected-range
registers before the payload has programmed them.

FSP's PostPciEnumeration notify locks the SPI controller even when its
lockdown UPDs are disabled. Skip that phase in SMM lockdown mode and run the
equivalent native PMC and TCO finalization instead.

The expected configuration is to unselect INTEL_CHIPSET_LOCKDOWN and select
SOC_INTEL_COMMON_SPI_LOCKDOWN_SMM.

Testing various microarchitectures happens on the Heads repository:
https://github.com/linuxboot/heads/pull/1818

TEST=Lock the SPI flash using APM_CNT_FINALIZE in Heads on Alder Lake
(Protectli VP66xx) and Comet Lake (Protectli VP46xx) platforms. Check if
flash is unlocked in the Heads recovery console. Check if flash is locked in
the kexeced OS.

Change-Id: Icbcc6fcde90e5b0a999aacb720e2e3dc2748c838

Signed-off-by: Michał Żygowski <michal.zygowski@3mdeb.com>

Signed-off-by: Sean Rhodes <sean@starlabs.systems>
